# .vault_pass.example — template for the vault password file
#
# ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
#  What this file is
# ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
#
# .vault_pass is a plain-text file containing the password used
# to encrypt and decrypt group_vars/vault.yml with ansible-vault.
#
# Ansible reads it automatically when run.sh finds it — no
# --ask-vault-pass prompt needed on subsequent runs.
#
# ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
#  Where it lives
# ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
#
# Place it in the root of this repository (next to ansible.cfg):
#
#   iac/
#   ├── ansible.cfg
#   ├── .vault_pass          ← here (gitignored)
#   ├── .vault_pass.example  ← this file (safe to commit)
#   └── ...
#
# ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
#  Setup
# ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
#
# 1. Copy this file:
#
#      cp .vault_pass.example .vault_pass
#
# 2. Replace the line below with your actual vault password:

your-vault-password-here

# 3. Restrict permissions so only your user can read it:
#
#      chmod 600 .vault_pass
#
# 4. Encrypt group_vars/vault.yml if not already done:
#
#      ansible-vault encrypt --vault-password-file .vault_pass group_vars/vault.yml
#
# run.sh picks up .vault_pass automatically from this point on.
#
# ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
#  Security
# ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
#
# .vault_pass is listed in .gitignore — it is never committed to git.
# Do not share it, store it in a password manager instead.
# If you suspect the password has been compromised, rekey the vault:
#
#   ansible-vault rekey --vault-password-file .vault_pass group_vars/vault.yml
